          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. Her later explanation and aside reinforce that the focal words are an avowal, irrespective of how Lear evaluates them.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal does not create a new promise. In its immediate continuation, however, Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond and later describes the duties she returns to Lear. Thus there is weak commitment-related force: Cordelia presents herself as fulfilling an existing daughterly obligation, not undertaking unlimited or exclusive future devotion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled answer explaining the measured extent of Cordelia’s love, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Her inability to make an extravagant declaration does not turn the focal avowal itself into an exclamation or involuntary outburst.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"No distinct residual core force is needed: the focal expression is adequately described as a direct avowal, with only a weak invocation of filial obligation supplied by its continuation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say who loves him most, linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained avowal rather than competing in extravagant praise. Lear treats it as inadequate and urges her to revise it to protect her fortunes.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia makes no clear new promise in the focal words. She invokes her existing filial bond and claims to return the duties fitting a daughter—obedience, love, and honour—while expressly rejecting unlimited love because a future husband would receive part of her love, care, and duty. Lear does not accept her formulation as a satisfactory answer; he asks her to “Mend your speech a little.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene of William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports classifying the utterance as an avowal and provides internal evidence of Cordelia’s affection. It does not fully specify the practical terms or future duration of the filial duties she invokes, and the focal wording alone would not establish a commitment without the surrounding references to bond and duty.\"}"
        }
